<h2>Two rounds lost, one left</h2>
<p>The petitioner&rsquo;s cheque — No. 157392, for Rs 15 lakh — bounced in 2019. The complaint (CC No. 34737/2019) ended in conviction on 31 October 2025. The first appeal (CA 795/2025, before the ASJ, Fast Track Court-01, South District, Saket) ended on 17 August 2026 with the conviction affirmed and the money liability recalculated: Rs 24,68,301, building in simple interest at 12%, payable in three months. The revision to the High Court is the third and narrowest tier — a jurisdiction confined to jurisdictional error and perversity, not a second appeal on facts.</p>

<h2>The interim architecture</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Delay condoned.</strong> A 24-day re-filing delay was condoned at the threshold.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Notice and record.</strong> Notice issued; the trial court record requisitioned; next date 12 January 2027.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conditional stay.</strong> The default sentence stays suspended only upon deposit of Rs 4,93,660 — 20% of the modified compensation, net of the Rs 1,50,000 already deposited — within four weeks.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Self-executing default.</strong> Miss the deposit, and the interim order vacates automatically, without a further hearing.</div>
</div>

<h2>The law the order applies</h2>
<p>Section 148 NI Act, introduced in 2018 to blunt the dilatory economics of cheque litigation, permits appellate courts to require the drawer to deposit at least 20% of the fine or compensation. The Supreme Court authorities cited in the order chart its reach: <em>Stanny Felix Pinto</em> on securing complainants pending challenge, <em>Jamboo Bhandari</em> holding that the deposit is the norm and that exemption demands recorded reasons in exceptional cases, and <em>Muskan Enterprises</em> on the interplay of these powers in proceedings beyond the first appeal. The Delhi High Court&rsquo;s own line, including <em>Usha Jewellers</em>, applies the same discipline at the revisional stage: a drawer who has lost twice and seeks suspension of the consequences must put real money where the challenge is. On this record — concurrent findings, a guarantor defence already rejected twice, and a reply to the statutory notice as the principal plank — no exceptional circumstance justified waiver.</p>

<h2>What drawers and payees should take from it</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>If you are the drawer</th><th>If you are the payee</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Budget for the 20% before filing the challenge; the stay is conditional and the default clause is automatic</td><td>Press for the deposit at the first hearing — it secures part of the award and tests the challenge&rsquo;s seriousness</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Amounts already deposited below are adjusted, so keep the trail of earlier deposits precise</td><td>Point the court to the modified, interest-inclusive figure as the base for the 20%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Waiver requires demonstrated exceptional circumstances — insolvency-level incapacity, not inconvenience</td><td>On default of deposit, move immediately; the vacation of stay needs no fresh adjudication</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The broader signal is consistent with the statute&rsquo;s purpose. Cheque prosecutions exist to make commercial paper trustworthy; interim orders that let a twice-convicted drawer litigate indefinitely at the payee&rsquo;s cost would restore exactly the incentives Parliament legislated against. The deposit regime keeps the revision alive while the payee&rsquo;s recovery stops bleeding.</p>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
